WEBVTT

00:00:00.517 --> 00:00:21.565
<v Casey>Morning, good to have you. It's Saturday, so it's Cariboo Local Affairs day. Today we've got Sparwood's new mayor, a 32-year Sparwood resident and retired miner who won in a landslide. We've got Ottawa's budget and what it leaves out for farms. And the Deep Dive: a post office that took years to arrive in Tŝideldel, and what a counter actually fixes. Let's get into it.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:56.328
<v Casey>Good morning, and welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Saturday, November 7th, 2026, and Remembrance Day is coming up on Wednesday. Today's theme is Cariboo Local Affairs. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:57.328 --> 00:01:04.416
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today we're on councils, budgets, and who gets a say, and the Deep Dive is about a post office that took years to arrive.

00:01:05.076 --> 00:01:27.748
<v Casey>Out at Horsefly Lake this morning, it's overcast and around minus 1, with a high of 9 and a low of minus 1 today. Light drizzle is expected through the day, with a 47 percent chance of precipitation, about 0 millimetres. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 1, Williams Lake and Quesnel are both around 0, and Redstone is around minus 1, all under overcast skies.

00:01:27.948 --> 00:01:28.652
<v Riley>On to the news.

00:01:30.052 --> 00:01:37.348
<v Riley>Most of today's news is about decisions made a long way from the people who live with them. A few stories land close to home.

00:01:39.078 --> 00:02:14.212
<v Riley>Sparwood, in southeastern B.C., has a new mayor. Hadden Kelloway is 65, a retired miner, and has lived in Sparwood for 32 years. He won the seat in a landslide on October 17th, in the 2026 municipal election. The Williams Lake Tribune reports he is the first mayor in B.C. to publicly come out as transgender. He began transitioning in 2011, and he says his campaign grew out of the support the community gave him during that process. Sparwood has about 4,200 residents and sits less than an hour from the Alberta border.

00:02:14.662 --> 00:02:20.534
<v Casey>He first moved there intending to stay a few years. Thirty-two years later, the town picked him for mayor.

00:02:21.998 --> 00:02:37.926
<v Riley>The Vancouver Sun went behind the scenes with party insiders and candidates to explain how the Conservatives won the B.C. election and why the NDP failed. Its sources are the people who ran the campaigns, and the story is about why each side ended up where it did.

00:02:39.478 --> 00:03:19.484
<v Casey>Ottawa's federal budget, tabled on Thursday, allows immediate expensing for farm machinery. It also widens the tax rollovers that move farms between family members. Farms were left out of the new $15-million capital gains incentive, according to RealAgriculture. The budget projects a federal deficit of $62.3 billion, or 1.8 per cent of GDP, for 2026-27, narrowing to $52.9 billion by 2030-31. Federal spending is projected above $602 billion in 2026-27, up 6.5 per cent on 2025-26. Interest payments are forecast to climb from $60.2 billion to $85.5 billion over the same period.

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:27.245
<v Riley>Immediate expensing helps the operation that already has the cash for the next machine. Farms missed the family-transfer incentive.

00:03:28.736 --> 00:03:38.328
<v Casey>About 1,200 miners are on strike at one of Canada's largest open-pit mines in B.C. The Tyee reports that long-running safety complaints sit behind the labour dispute.

00:03:39.835 --> 00:04:31.761
<v Riley>Five U.S. agencies warned on November 5th that hackers are targeting industrial refrigeration and cold-chain controllers worldwide. The targets include controllers used by grocery stores, hospitals, food producers and transport operators. Attackers may be able to change temperatures or defrost settings, or stop operators from seeing the readings. European supermarkets were attacked on November 2nd, and an incident altered refrigeration settings at three French blood-service facilities. The agencies did not say whether systems in the United States had been targeted. Their advice is to remove unnecessary internet access, secure remote connections, update firmware, and keep control systems off business networks. U.S. investigators are also examining August refrigeration malfunctions that affected at least six military commissaries.

00:04:32.211 --> 00:04:36.003
<v Casey>A remote defrost setting is convenient until somebody else has the login.

00:04:36.453 --> 00:04:44.397
<v Riley>EuroCommerce's director general said affected products should be withdrawn if a cold-chain disruption leaves their safety uncertain.

00:04:45.844 --> 00:05:14.644
<v Casey>Maclean's looks at Canada's Hutterite colonies, where smartphones and social media are blamed for phone addiction and subversive ideas. The piece follows Giselle Waldner, a 29-year-old Hutterite who lives in rural Manitoba. Her YouTube channel has 46,000 followers. It shows baking, berry-picking, window-washing and scripture reading, all done on what the magazine calls an industrial scale. The headline dek says how these communities handle disruptive technology has lessons for everyone else.

00:05:16.234 --> 00:05:46.666
<v Riley>Passive House Plus, drawing on research by architect and academic Joseph Little, says timber counter battens could significantly cut moisture risk in many pitched-roof retrofits in the UK and Ireland. Counter battens run vertically along the rafters, above the roof underlay. They're standard practice across much of northern Europe, and they're almost never used in the UK and Ireland. They add relatively little cost or complexity. Little's example is a retrofit of a Victorian mid-terrace cottage.

00:05:47.116 --> 00:05:53.604
<v Casey>The evidence is British and Irish housing. Whether it transfers to a Cariboo roof in winter is a question for a local builder.

00:05:55.136 --> 00:06:40.270
<v Riley>Canada and China extended tariff relief through the end of 2028 on canola meal, peas, lobster and crab. Canadian pork is left out, and it still faces a 25 per cent Chinese tariff. The Canadian Meat Council estimates the duties have cost processors about $126 million over 18 months. Farm Progress covered the same deal, which also extends tariff remission on certain Chinese steel and aluminum products. From March through September, Canadian growers and exporters shipped 975,000 metric tons of peas to China during the temporary relief, worth $421 million. Prime Minister Mark Carney is scheduled to attend the APEC summit in Shenzhen on November 18th and 19th.

00:06:41.728 --> 00:07:17.758
<v Casey>MIT engineers cast a three-metre test beam in a sewn fabric mold. Compared with a conventional beam built to carry the same load, it used about a third as much concrete and half as much steel reinforcement. The fabric molds come from an automated pattern-making process, so the method avoids the specialized equipment, such as concrete 3D printers or machine-carved molds, that made similar shapes hard to build. The team's measured response came within about 2 percent of its computer model. The paper appears in the Journal of Building Engineering, and the researchers pointed to regions where new construction is expected to grow.

00:07:18.208 --> 00:07:21.320
<v Riley>A mold you can sew is a mold a small shop can make.

00:07:22.768 --> 00:08:00.014
<v Casey>Romania's agriculture minister, Florin Barbu, opposes a proposed €100,000 cap on EU farm subsidies. He also opposes merging farm funding into a single fund under the Common Agricultural Policy for 2028 to 2034. He wants direct payments and rural development funding kept separate, and he said Romania would not support the regulation if the cap is adopted. A presidential adviser, Radu Burnete, said a draft would begin reducing payments at €100,000 and raise the ceiling to €300,000. He also said the proposals are not final, and that Romania's 6 per cent budget deficit makes co-financing difficult.

00:08:01.501 --> 00:08:36.011
<v Riley>A new study estimates snakebite envenoming kills between 274,000 and 513,000 people a year, far above official figures. The authors built a geostatistical model on data from 79 countries, combining real-world records with environmental and social information to fill the gaps. Over half the countries with venomous snakes don't count bites at all. In rural sub-Saharan Africa and south Asia, many victims live hours from a clinic, lack transport, or see traditional healers, so their cases never reach national health statistics.

00:08:37.588 --> 00:09:08.370
<v Casey>An AARP study, shared first with NPR, finds family caregivers spend an average of $11,508 a year out of pocket. That's about 60 per cent more than five years ago, and 31 per cent of their income on average. Inflation over the same five years was 24 per cent, according to the federal consumer price index. The study surveyed 2,538 family caregivers. Food, housing and medical costs make up the biggest share, and the growth tracks the extra care and more complicated medical work that families take on at home.

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:12.204
<v Riley>The cost rises with the care. Nobody budgets for that part.

00:09:13.626 --> 00:09:49.472
<v Casey>The U.S. State Department approved a proposed $2.1 billion sale of 500 Patriot PAC-3 interceptors to Ukraine, subject to congressional review. Canada, Germany and Norway will contribute through JUMPSTART, a U.S. program that lets allies fund arms purchases, alongside EU Ukraine Support Loan funds. The State Department says no U.S. funds would be used. Lockheed Martin is the principal contractor. Two people briefed on the decision told Al Jazeera they don't expect deliveries until the early 2030s. NATO's commander, Gen. Alexus Grynkewich, said U.S. shipments were ongoing, and that one had arrived in the week before.

00:09:49.922 --> 00:09:54.298
<v Riley>So Canada's money buys a place in a line that moves in years, not months.

00:09:56.099 --> 00:10:27.523
<v Riley>Two new surveys show nearly two-thirds of tweens and teens use AI chatbots. Most use them for schoolwork, and many use them for companionship and emotional support. Common Sense Media found nearly half of 9-to-12-year-olds have used a chatbot or companion, and nearly three-quarters of 13-year-olds have. The JED Foundation surveyed 5,500 9-to-17-year-olds in schools, and found 90 per cent have used one. Some of those uses are linked with worse mental health symptoms.

00:10:27.973 --> 00:10:31.197
<v Casey>Two advocacy groups, two surveys, and no regulator's numbers.

00:10:32.843 --> 00:11:06.561
<v Casey>A builder took the mainboard out of a Framework Laptop 13 and turned it into a Proxmox server. He disconnected the display, keyboard and battery, then powered the board through USB-C and started it with the board's own power button. The build runs a Ryzen 7 7840U with 16 gigabytes of DDR5 memory. Framework designed that board to run as a standalone computer, so the project reuses the hardware for the second life it was built for. The processor has eight cores and 16 threads, but thread count alone doesn't settle how many virtual machines the setup can carry.

00:11:07.011 --> 00:11:11.571
<v Riley>Eight cores, sixteen threads, and memory you can swap. That's the part worth buying.

00:11:12.021 --> 00:11:15.333
<v Casey>Next, a volunteer team that answers when people need help.

00:11:16.733 --> 00:11:40.429
<v Riley>Central Cariboo Search and Rescue is a team of highly trained volunteer first responders. They serve the central Cariboo locally, regionally, and provincially, and they're ready to respond when the community needs them. Remembrance Day is Wednesday, a day to honour those who served. Central Cariboo Search and Rescue carries that same sense of service into every call. Find them at ccsar.ca.

00:11:41.082 --> 00:11:46.242
<v Casey>Volunteers who show up in the worst hours are the service nobody notices until they're needed.

00:11:46.518 --> 00:11:50.806
<v Riley>Next, we head to the Chilcotin for a post office that took years to arrive.

00:11:52.206 --> 00:12:03.982
<v Casey>A post office is a counter, some boxes and a postmark. For decades, residents of Tŝideldel collected their mail in neighbouring Chilanko Forks, under another community's name. So what does a counter actually fix?

00:12:04.263 --> 00:12:13.023
<v Riley>The address, first. Mail for that place finally carries the place's own name. That sounds small until you're the one living under somebody else's town name.

00:12:13.671 --> 00:12:22.607
<v Casey>Clayton Charleyboy put it plainly: "So our identity was tied to somewhere we didn't live." A postmark for a place you don't live in is a very patient kind of confusion.

00:12:23.266 --> 00:12:34.538
<v Riley>He also said they've been displaced a few times, and Tŝideldel is the last place they ended up. A counter, in that context, is a kind of settling. The address is just the public part.

00:12:35.194 --> 00:12:51.186
<v Casey>The opening was November 2nd, at 710 Redstone Flats, after years of work by the Tŝideldel Development Corporation and Canada Post. But a councillor facing a colleague's deal has the same choice these people did: press quietly, or force it into the open. Which did Tŝideldel do?

00:12:51.477 --> 00:13:05.749
<v Riley>Press, for years, and keep the relationship intact. That's the case for keeping the peace: a federal body doesn't move on a whim, and a community that stays civil keeps the file open. An address and a date. That's a result you can check.

00:13:06.435 --> 00:13:22.219
<v Casey>Prince George Daily News reports that Chilanko Forks lost its postal service in 1918 and regained it in 1963. That office has served the surrounding communities, Tŝideldel included, for decades. So the gap was never the mail. It was the name on the mail.

00:13:22.795 --> 00:13:28.027
<v Riley>Which is the point. The service existed in the area. What changed is whose name it carries.

00:13:28.666 --> 00:13:48.242
<v Casey>The Tribune's opening photo caption names five people: Clayton Charleyboy, John Smith, Chief Otis Guichon Sr., Kevin Ned and Harjot Dhaliwal. It places representatives from Canada Post and Tŝideldel First Nation at the opening. The years in between aren't in the photo. What did Canada Post ask for in all those meetings? What did the community commit to?

00:13:48.523 --> 00:13:55.203
<v Riley>Then ask for it. Publish the agreement. A photo proves the door opened. It doesn't show how the decision was made.

00:13:55.512 --> 00:13:58.536
<v Casey>And an address doesn't create jobs, broadband, or a clinic.

00:13:59.084 --> 00:14:09.100
<v Riley>Nobody claimed it did. It's the first thing a bank, a benefits office or a delivery driver checks. Getting that right is the precondition for everything after it.

00:14:09.702 --> 00:14:18.678
<v Casey>Then count the costs. Every trip to a neighbour's counter is a cost, and nobody puts that in the announcement. Who in Tŝideldel couldn't easily make that trip before?

00:14:18.953 --> 00:14:28.673
<v Riley>The people closest to the new counter, for a start. For anyone further out, the forwarding form is the bridge, and that's a process question more than a distance one.

00:14:29.211 --> 00:14:47.867
<v Casey>The Tribune reports a one-year window. Residents can complete a form at either the Chilanko Forks office or the Tŝideldel office, get a new postal box and have their mail forwarded. That's a year with two addresses in circulation. When a parcel lands at the wrong counter, who does a resident call? Canada Post, the corporation, or the council?

00:14:48.211 --> 00:14:56.395
<v Riley>Put the end date and a named staff contact on the form. A transition with a deadline and a name is checkable. Without them, it's a hope.

00:14:56.909 --> 00:15:03.453
<v Casey>If residents move their business to the new counter, does the Chilanko Forks office lose volume, and does its staffing hold?

00:15:03.721 --> 00:15:10.521
<v Riley>That's a real cost, and the forwarding window is the place to measure it. Count the forms filed at each office over the year.

00:15:11.167 --> 00:15:18.823
<v Casey>Pressing an existing institution works until the other side says no. What does a Chilcotin community do when the federal partner won't come?

00:15:19.133 --> 00:15:47.757
<v Riley>Sometimes you build. In Lancaster, Pennsylvania, a Mennonite community tired of waiting for biopharma is building a drug company of its own, for maple syrup urine disease, which is unusually common there. Kevin Strauss has spent two decades treating the affected families. A gene therapy worked in a calf named Petunia. Her brother Louie died within hours of birth, and the researchers kept Petunia alive over two sleepless nights before injecting her with the therapy.

00:15:48.437 --> 00:15:57.965
<v Casey>A calf. The first goal was proving the therapy could work at all. Offering it to families comes after, and the community carries that risk with a few researchers out front.

00:15:58.529 --> 00:16:06.553
<v Riley>That's the build route's price. It works when the need is concentrated and nobody else is coming. Tŝideldel had a door to knock on, and it knocked.

00:16:07.225 --> 00:16:15.385
<v Casey>The knocking route depends on luck. It worked because Canada Post was willing this time, and nothing in the piece says that willingness is standard.

00:16:16.034 --> 00:16:28.186
<v Riley>It isn't luck when a corporation does the groundwork for years. A federal body doesn't move on a whim, so a community that keeps the file open and the relationship warm is what turns a no into a yes.

00:16:28.764 --> 00:16:36.532
<v Casey>Then the strategy is unrecorded. The years of work get one line in a news story. Who kept the file open, and who decided when to keep knocking?

00:16:36.809 --> 00:16:39.849
<v Riley>Those are the people who should be named in the minutes.

00:16:40.501 --> 00:16:44.397
<v Casey>Knocking is cheaper than building, but it puts the weight on whoever does the knocking.

00:16:44.944 --> 00:17:02.592
<v Riley>Clayton Charleyboy is an elected Tŝideldel First Nation councillor and the executive director of the Development Corporation. An elected councillor running the corporation is one way a small nation keeps its development body answerable to residents. Voters can see who holds the seat.

00:17:03.139 --> 00:17:18.011
<v Casey>Nothing is alleged. But one person on both sides of the table is a structure, and I'd want to know who else had a say. Who picks the board? Who can remove them? And if someone had objected along the way, would it have been voiced, or swallowed to keep the project moving?

00:17:18.296 --> 00:17:25.336
<v Riley>Those answers belong in bylaws and minutes. Publish them, and the record does the answering, not the opening day photo.

00:17:25.516 --> 00:17:26.644
<v Casey>Minutes, not photos.

00:17:27.323 --> 00:17:34.715
<v Riley>Charleyboy said the community is far from major cities. A counter in a place like that is a service that doesn't arrive on its own.

00:17:35.362 --> 00:17:47.290
<v Casey>So a no from Canada Post costs more out there. The neighbour's counter is the backup, and the backup still carries somebody else's name. The address is symbolic and the counter is practical, and neither one proves the other.

00:17:47.828 --> 00:17:57.444
<v Riley>Identity is a service. A resident who can state where they live without explaining it is a resident the system can find. The address is part of the infrastructure.

00:17:57.977 --> 00:18:04.705
<v Casey>Maybe. But you can't check an identity the way you check a box count. We have the box count. The identity isn't tested yet.

00:18:05.311 --> 00:18:12.767
<v Riley>Then the test is the forwarding window. If the mail moves to the new name in a year, the identity is working as infrastructure.

00:18:13.422 --> 00:18:23.486
<v Casey>CBC News reports this is the Tŝideldel First Nation's first Canada Post office. First makes a good headline. Who gets the next one, and does the next community have to knock as long?

00:18:23.826 --> 00:18:31.714
<v Riley>Then it's a template. Ask Canada Post and the corporation how long the wait ran, and what the conditions were. Publish the answers.

00:18:32.274 --> 00:18:42.922
<v Casey>And the money. The article doesn't say who paid for the space or the staffing. A post office is a lease, a counter and somebody's wage every week. Who carries that line after the opening photo?

00:18:43.206 --> 00:18:50.390
<v Riley>That's the question to put to Canada Post, and it has a checkable answer. Ask for the staffing plan and the lease term.

00:18:50.903 --> 00:18:59.415
<v Casey>Three parties were in the room. The corporation keeps what the community asked for. The council keeps what it decided. Canada Post keeps what it agreed to.

00:19:00.056 --> 00:19:04.616
<v Riley>Which is how records go missing. Nobody is lying. Each one assumes somebody else filed it.

00:19:05.252 --> 00:19:13.980
<v Casey>A Cariboo community could check that a year from now. Whether all three records exist in one place is the real test, and I don't know that anyone holds them together.

00:19:14.590 --> 00:19:30.846
<v Riley>The minimum is simple. Ask for the agreement, the minutes and the forwarding deadline, and keep copies in the community, not only in the office that filed them. If you have paperwork behind a win in your own community, send it to feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a.

00:19:31.128 --> 00:19:33.840
<v Casey>Read the minutes first, and enjoy your Remembrance Day.

00:19:34.440 --> 00:19:53.616
